## Prevalence and symptoms of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome

Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) is a common endocrine disorder affecting approximately 6-15% of women of reproductive age. It is characterized by symptoms such as irregular menstrual cycles, hirsutism, and hyperandrogenism. The syndrome's complexity arises from its various manifestations, which can significantly impact women's health and quality of life. [Data: Entities (3880, 5441); Relationships (12724, 12047)]

## Association with obesity

Obesity is frequently associated with Polycystic Ovary Syndrome, exacerbating its symptoms and leading to further health complications. Weight gain can worsen insulin resistance and hormonal imbalances, making management of PCOS more challenging. This relationship underscores the importance of addressing weight in the treatment and management of PCOS. [Data: Relationships (5854, 8056, 8057)]

## Increased risk of Diabetes Mellitus Type 2

Women with Polycystic Ovary Syndrome are at a heightened risk of developing Diabetes Mellitus Type 2. This connection is primarily due to insulin resistance, which is prevalent in PCOS patients. Understanding this risk is crucial for early intervention and management strategies to prevent the onset of diabetes. [Data: Relationships (12714, 13258)]

## Hyperandrogenism as a key feature

Hyperandrogenism is a defining characteristic of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome, leading to symptoms such as hirsutism and acne. Elevated androgen levels can significantly affect women's physical appearance and psychological well-being, making it a critical area of focus in PCOS management. [Data: Relationships (12724, 11099, 12047)]

